{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Hypothesis-driven latency investigation — findings (git-history + 5 experiments)\n\nInvestigated \"what changed in the last week that makes the gates slow\" against the recent\n`src/rebar/llm` history and the in-band telemetry (`.rebar/usage.jsonl`, 208 calls after\nthe two live A/B reviews). Ran experiments to prove/disprove each candidate. **Confirmed\nmechanism is provider-independent; the Anthropic→Bedrock migration correlates in time but is\nNOT the cause.**\n\n## Confirmed mechanism (H6)\nWall-clock is dominated by **output-token generation volume across many agentic turns**, with\na stable decode rate:\n- `corr(duration, output_tokens) = 0.982`; `corr(duration, output/request) = 0.952` — far above\n  `corr(duration, input_tokens) = 0.789` and `corr(duration, requests) = 0.807`.\n- Decode rate is **~60 tok/s and stable** (single-turn ops 53–89 tok/s; agentic per-request\n  median 66 tok/s, min 41 / max 81) — i.e. **no throttle/retry stalls**. Latency ≈\n  Σ_passes Σ_turns(output_tokens)/60.\nSo the cost is the **design** of the code-grounding review: multi-pass (per-criterion finder\nfan-out → 106 `plan-reviewer` calls) × tool-using agentic verify (`plan-review-verifier-agentic`\nup to 31 turns / 236s / 1.4M cumulative input tokens). This predates the migration.\n\n## Disproved candidates (each with its experiment)\n- **H5 — provider raw latency: DISPROVED.** Controlled A/B on ONE plan (epic 992d, per-class\n  `REBAR_LLM_<CLASS>_MODEL` overrides): single-shot `overlap-judge` bed **5.06s** vs ant **5.22s**\n  (within noise, identical tokens/cache); full agentic review bed **428.7s** vs ant **903.0s** —\n  Anthropic was *slower*. Provider is not the driver.\n- **H1 — repeated/runaway tool calls: DISPROVED.** Across all 95 multi-request calls\n  `max_consecutive_repeat = 1` and `tool_calls_distinct ≈ tool_calls` (939/941). The recent\n  loop-control cluster (`c827 abort runaway loops`, `f6fc memoize duplicate reads`,\n  `windowed distinct-ratio signal`) already suppresses repetition; the turns are distinct work.\n- **H3 — `30a2` model-max output budget (Jul 30): DISPROVED as a latency cause.** All 208 calls\n  finished `finish_reason=stop`; **zero `length` truncations**; no single request approached even\n  the *old* 16k per-turn ceiling (the 2 calls whose *cumulative* output >16k were 19- and 25-turn\n  agentic calls, ~900–1000 tok/turn). `effective_max_tokens=max(floor,requested)` is only a\n  ceiling, so raising 16k→model-max did not increase output here. 30a2 is a correctness fix\n  (prevents rare INDETERMINATE-on-truncation), not a regression.\n- **H4 — Bedrock `PromptedOutput` fallback (`895c`, old botocore): NOT a latency driver.** Decode\n  rate is provider-independent and stable, so the fallback's extra parse/log noise doesn't move\n  wall-clock vs Anthropic native output. (It remains a correctness/observability risk — logs\n  `multiple candidates validated … selecting the LAST` — but not a latency cause.)\n- **H2 — quadratic input-context growth: real but NOT dominant.** `tok/req` stays roughly flat\n  (~10–15k) because prompt caching absorbs the growing prefix (`cache_read` 50–89% of input every\n  turn). Input context grows, but it is cheap and generates the weaker correlation (0.789).\n\n## What actually changed in the window (H7 — partial contributors, ranked)\nThe dominant cost is the pre-existing agentic design; recent commits add turns/output only at the\nmargin:\n1. **`ff64` (Jul 30) — T1 web grounding via a real `web_search` tool.** Adds a network tool-call\n   turn to T1-routed passes (genuine turn-adder, but scoped to one criterion).\n2. **Criteria enabled to BLOCKING (Aug 5): `G7`, `T3/T10`+T10 DET gate, `T5c` HIGH-class, `T11`.**\n   Deepen Pass-2 verification (more verify objects → more output).\n3. **New criteria `T15`, `P10/P11` (Jul 30–Aug 5):** +2 criteria (41→43), small finder fan-out add.\n4. **NOT regressions:** `30a2` (no-op ceiling here), `9374` plan-first shared prefix + `1dbe`/`dd27`\n   caching (speedups), the Bedrock cutover itself (not slower per token).\n\n## Conclusion\nThe minutes-scale wall-clock is the **inherent, provider-independent cost of the agentic\ncode-grounding review** (output volume × turns × per-criterion passes). The Anthropic→Bedrock\nmigration is temporally coincident — it shipped in the same window as the criteria-enablement /\nweb-grounding / exhaustiveness changes and made Bedrock (with the 895c prompted-output correctness\ndegradation) the default — but Bedrock is **not slower per token**, so it does not explain the\nlatency.\n\n## Confirmatory experiment deliberately NOT run (rationale)\nA before/after bisect (identical review at a pre-Jul-30 checkout) would further quantify the\nmarginal contributors, but the baseline predates the `passes.py` completion-seam split (`8705`)\nand the model-classes / Bedrock provider seam, so an old build carries schema/provider drift that\nwould confound an output-volume comparison. The five experiments above already isolate the\nmechanism provider-independently, so the bisect is a nice-to-have, not load-bearing.\n\n## Actionable levers for the eventual fix (the real cost centers)\n- Cut **turns/output in the agentic verifier** (the 31-turn / 236s cost center): tighten the\n  per-criterion tool-exploration budget, or batch criteria per verify call (fewer, cheaper turns).\n- Reduce **per-criterion finder fan-out** (106 `plan-reviewer` calls) — batch more criteria per\n  finder call (partially done by `730ff31 batch overlap-judge`, extend to the finder).\n- Restore per-pass latency telemetry on the sidecar (task 4a40) so this is measured in-band, and\n  upgrade botocore ≥1.43.64 to drop the 895c prompted-output overhead (correctness, not latency)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786077763136645001,"uuid":"85527f4b-14e6-4999-99b0-011ea69501d5"}